WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.560
Fill an excised lung with saline instead of air, and it takes far less pressure to

2
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.020
inflate it to the same volume.

3
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.820
The tissue is the same both times.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:19.940
In 1929, Kurt von Neergaard compared an isolated pig lung filled with air and with a

5
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:25.600
gum solution. The liquid removes the air-liquid surface that lines every alveolus.

6
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.640
He concluded that surface tension, not tissue elasticity, accounts for most of the

7
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:32.640
lung's recoil.

8
00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.043
So how hard is it to keep one alveolus open?

9
00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:41.063
Laplace's relation answers it for a single spherical air-liquid surface.

10
00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:44.203
The pressure equals 2T divided by r.

11
00:00:44.723 --> 00:00:47.983
T is the surface tension, and r is the radius.

12
00:00:48.823 --> 00:00:54.243
Take a radius of 100 micrometres and a tension of 70 millinewtons per metre with no

13
00:00:54.243 --> 00:00:56.983
surfactant. These are illustrative values.

14
00:00:57.543 --> 00:01:00.343
The pressure is 14.3 centimetres of water.

15
00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:04.543
Now halve the radius to 50 micrometres, at the same tension.

16
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:08.623
The pressure doubles, to 28.6 centimetres of water.

17
00:01:09.223 --> 00:01:11.343
Join the two alveoli to one airway.

18
00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:15.663
At equal tension, the small alveolus would empty into the large one.

19
00:01:16.303 --> 00:01:19.303
Surface tension makes the smaller space harder to hold open.

20
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:21.777
Surfactant changes that.

21
00:01:22.677 --> 00:01:28.057
In the 1950s, John Clements put films from rat, cat and dog lung extracts on a

22
00:01:28.057 --> 00:01:32.857
surface balance. A movable barrier compresses the film, and a thin plate hanging in

23
00:01:32.857 --> 00:01:34.317
the surface reports the tension.

24
00:01:34.997 --> 00:01:36.497
The tension depended on area.

25
00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:42.017
It fell from about 46 to about 10 dynes per centimetre as the film was compressed.

26
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:45.217
He named the material pulmonary surfactant.

27
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.270
Give the alveoli a lining whose tension falls as the area falls.

28
00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:56.110
These values are illustrative, and the tension in the small alveolus is made up.

29
00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:01.910
At 100 micrometres, with surfactant, tension is 20 millinewtons per metre.

30
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.450
The pressure is 4.1 centimetres of water.

31
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:10.090
At 50 micrometres, tension is 5 millinewtons per metre.

32
00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.290
The pressure is 2.0 below the large alveolus.

33
00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.590
The small one no longer empties.

34
00:02:17.283 --> 00:02:21.483
This emptying is a result of theory and of films studied in vitro.

35
00:02:22.143 --> 00:02:26.483
Whether a film prevents it depends on how steeply tension changes with area.

36
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:29.183
Real alveoli are also tethered.

37
00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:33.863
Neighbours share walls, so one alveolus cannot shrink alone like a free bubble.

38
00:02:34.343 --> 00:02:39.463
Keep this rule. Surface tension makes small alveoli harder to hold open, and

39
00:02:39.463 --> 00:02:42.883
surfactant lowers tension most when the alveolus is small.
